If you're capturing interrupts, I suspect it's useful to include a get-out-of-jail free card like this:

```R
check_repeated_interrupt <- function() {
  if (Sys.time() - the$last_interrupt < 1) {
    cli::cli_alert_warning("Interrupting")
    interrupt()
  }
  the$last_interrupt <- Sys.time()
}
```

Otherwise if that function is nested inside a loop, or worse another interrupt capturing function, you really have to hammer Ctrl + C to get an interrupt at exactly the right time.

I hit this in ellmer where I had a loop around `httr2::req_perform_parallel()` and I found it very hard to escape from.

Handling error calls to print meaningful error messages can be relatively tedious when developing packages. This is particularly true when having custom checker functions being executed inside nested functions or an object/function that produces many frames such as with an R6 class or `map_*()`/`walk_*()` functions. Passing the relevant call to every function is tedious and often doesn't seem appropriate. Unless I missed something, `local_error_call()` doesn't seem to help much either in these cases.

A simple solution would be to automatically retrieve the caller name by finding the first frame in the call stack whose top environment belongs to the package namespace:

```{r}
user_call <- function() {
  ns <- ns_env()
  for (frame in as.list(sys.frames())) {
    if (identical(topenv(frame), ns)) {
      return(frame)
    }
  }
}
```

Then we can simply use that function as the default `call` argument in a wrapper function around `abort()` or `cli::cli_abort()` to print the relevant caller in the error message independently of where the error occurs in the call stack.

I can't really think of any real downside of this (maybe you know some). In my opinion, that would be useful to package developers to simplify the handling of error calls in some cases. What do you think?

My understanding from the documentation is that these should behave in the same way, but I have found at least one (slightly convoluted) example where this is not the case.

(this is in R 4.4.1 with rlang 1.1.4)

```

simpler_inside <- function(c,d){
  c+d
}

simpler <- function(...){
  ldots <- list(...)
  rlang::inject(
    simpler_inside(
      !!!ldots
    )
  )
}
simpler(3,4)
```

This works, but if you replace `simpler` with

```
simpler <- function(...){
  ldots <- list(...)
  rlang::inject(
    simpler_inside(
      rlang::splice(ldots)
    )
  )
}
simpler(3,4)
```

This now errors with `Error in simpler_inside(rlang::splice(ldots)) : argument "d" is missing, with no default`. Looking at the debug, it seems like in this example the "splice" call is passed as the first argument to `simpler_fun` and then unpacks, and ideally should unpack before that. 

I appreciate this example is a little artificial (there's no reason for me to turn the dots into a list here); my actual example involves a function factory where I need to extract the dots to pass to a function. 

Perhaps this is me not understanding the differences, but I had thought that splice and !!! should act in the same way here.

